-- title:
--   ∞̄-chart integers are the localised modular ring
-- statement:
--   Let $p$ be a prime, let $A$ be a valuation subring of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}} =$ `AlgebraicClosure ℚ` whose residue field has characteristic $p$ (and carries decidable equality), and let $\Gamma$ be a chart context `ChartCtx p A`: a package consisting of modular polynomial data at $p$ together with a Kronecker congruence for it, integrality of the two level-one Hecke maps $\bar\alpha,\bar\beta$ at $p$, a place specialisation $P$ of level $1$ from $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-places to places over the residue field of $A$ along `IsLocalRing.residue`, a level-one prolongation pair $R$ for $P$, a set $S_1$ of places of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}(X_0(1\cdot p))$, a finite set $W_n$ of places of the characteristic-$p$ level-one modular function field equal to the supersingular places, finiteness and cardinality data for the supersingular $j$-set, and a chart supply for $R$ on $S_1$. For $f$ in $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-modular function field `modularFunctionFieldBar (1 * p)`, realised inside $\mathrm{LaurentSeries}(\overline{\mathbb{Q}})$, the assertion is: $f$ lies in the valuation subring `integers` of the component chart `infChart Γ` (the first chart attached to $\Gamma.R$, $\Gamma.S_1$, $\Gamma.W_n$ and the supply) if and only if the underlying Laurent series of $f$ lies in `CharPReduction.modularLocalized (1 * p) A.toSubring (IsLocalRing.residue ↥A)`, i.e. it can be written as $r/s$ with $r,s$ in the subring of $\mathrm{LaurentSeries}(\overline{\mathbb{Q}})$ generated by the constant series with coefficients in $A$ together with the $q$-expansions `jqModC` and `jqNModC` at level $1\cdot p$, the denominator $s$ having nonzero coefficientwise reduction modulo the maximal ideal of $A$.
--
--   This identifies the ring of integers of the $\bar\infty$-chart of the multiplicative covering of $X_0(p)$ at $p$ with the localisation of $A[j,j_p]$, viewed through $q$-expansions at the cusp $\infty$, at the primitive (coefficientwise nonzero mod $\mathfrak{m}_A$) denominators. It is the interface statement by which later constructions — the Frobenius computation on residues of the zero chart, the production of family data from bifiltered digits, and the existence of a uniform multiplicative covering with a certified family for primes $p \ge 5$ — recognise membership in the $\bar\infty$-chart by a $q$-expansion criterion.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_ModularCurve_MultCovering_mem_infChart_integers_iff_coe_mem_modularLocalized.lean

